[*.cs]

# CS8600: Null リテラルまたは Null の可能性がある値を Null 非許容型に変換しています。
#
# Keep it. Measured 2026-08-27: removing this line surfaces 446 occurrences and nothing else,
# all one shape -- a local declared non-nullable and assigned a value that may be null, then
# checked afterwards:
#
#     Exception ret = null;
#     string folder = Path.GetDirectoryName(configFilePath);
#     object values = fakeBoundParameters[parameterName];
#
# The obvious worry is that suppressing CS8600 also blinds the compiler downstream -- that the
# local is thereafter believed non-null and a later dereference goes unchecked. It does not.
# Measured with a probe: `string s = Maybe(); return s.Length;` reports CS8602 exactly as
# `string? s = Maybe();` does. The flow state follows the value, not the declaration.
#
# So writing `?` on 446 declarations would buy nothing but self-documentation, while the
# diagnostics that catch real faults -- CS8602 dereference, CS8603 return, CS8604 argument --
# are unaffected and are build errors (see UiPathOrch.csproj). Recorded here so the next reader
# does not re-run either experiment.
#
# This line alone is NOT enough. <WarningsAsErrors>nullable</WarningsAsErrors> in
# UiPathOrch.csproj overrides an editorconfig severity of none and promotes CS8600 anyway
# (measured: 223 errors on a clean build), so the csproj also carries
# <WarningsNotAsErrors>CS8600</WarningsNotAsErrors>. Both are needed; changing one without the
# other breaks the build. The rest of the nullable family (CS8602 dereference, CS8603 return,
# CS8604 argument, CS8618 uninitialized, ...) is enforced as errors.
dotnet_diagnostic.CS8600.severity = none
